CHUNK({"chunk":"V","module":"m6","topics":[{"id":"m6-indian-acts","title":"Indian environmental Acts and Rules","short":"Indian Acts & Rules","blurb":"Every Indian Act and Rule in the syllabus with its year, what triggered it, what it set up and why it matters in the exam.","source":"Collected from Lectures W2–W4 (Water, Air, Noise, Solid waste, EPA, Forest and Wildlife Acts)","tabs":[{"title":"Quick table","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">Learn these as <b>year + one-line purpose + body it created</b>. Most MCQs ask \"which Act / which year / which body\".</p>\n          <table class=\"tbl\">\n            <thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>Act / Rule</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td><b>1972</b></td><td>Wildlife (Protection) Act</td><td>Protects wild animals, birds and plants through <b>Schedules</b> (Schedule I = highest protection); bans hunting of scheduled species; creates sanctuaries and national parks. India's domestic tool for <b>CITES</b>. Protects the <b>inhabitants</b>.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>1974</b> (enacted 23 March 1974)</td><td>Water (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act</td><td>India's <b>first comprehensive pollution law</b>. Set up the <b>CPCB (Section 3)</b> and <b>SPCBs (Section 4)</b>; consent needed before discharging effluent (Sections 24–26). Amended 1988 (stronger penalties).</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>1980</b></td><td>Forest (Conservation) Act</td><td><b>Section 2</b>: no non-forest use of forest land without <b>prior Central Government approval</b>; took the power away from states; compensatory afforestation (CAMPA). Protects the <b>habitat</b>. Amended 1988, 1992, 2023.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>1981</b></td><td>Air (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act</td><td>Gave the CPCB/SPCBs power over <b>air</b> pollution; declares air pollution control areas. Inspired by the 1972 Stockholm Conference. <b>1987 amendment added noise</b> as an air pollutant.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>1986</b> (passed 19 Nov 1986)</td><td>Environment (Protection) Act (EPA)</td><td><b>Umbrella law</b>: lets the Centre make rules and standards for <b>all</b> parts of the environment. Direct response to the <b>Bhopal Gas Tragedy (1984)</b> + Stockholm (1972).</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>2000</b></td><td>Noise Pollution (Regulation and Control) Rules</td><td>Ambient noise limits for industrial, commercial, residential and silence zones; loudspeaker limits at night. Made under the EPA 1986.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>2002</b></td><td>Biological Diversity Act</td><td>Implements the <b>CBD (1992)</b> in India: conservation, sustainable use and <b>fair sharing of benefits</b> from biological resources (National Biodiversity Authority).</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>2010</b></td><td>National Green Tribunal (NGT) Act</td><td>Set up the <b>NGT</b>, a specialised fast-track court for environment cases; appeals under the Water Act (Section 33B) go to it.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>2016</b></td><td>Solid Waste Management Rules</td><td>Made <b>segregation at source</b> mandatory (wet, dry, hazardous); duties on generators and local bodies.</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n          <div class=\"note exam\">Order to remember: <b>Wildlife 72 → Water 74 → Forest 80 → Air 81 → EPA 86</b>. Water came first, so <b>the Water Act created the CPCB</b>, and the Air Act reused it.</div>\n        "},{"title":"Why each matters","html":"\n          <ul>\n            <li><b>Wildlife Act 1972 + Forest Act 1980</b> are the <b>two-shield system</b>: Forest Act guards the habitat, Wildlife Act guards the animals and plants in it.</li>\n            <li><b>Water Act 1974</b> is the base of India's pollution-control system: CPCB at the national level, SPCBs in each state, the consent system, and penalties.</li>\n            <li><b>Air Act 1981</b> extended the same boards to air; the <b>1987 amendment</b> brought noise in.</li>\n            <li><b>EPA 1986</b> filled the gaps: one law for everything the earlier Acts missed (hazardous chemicals, industrial safety). Most later Rules (Noise 2000, SWM 2016) are made under it.</li>\n            <li><b>Biological Diversity Act 2002</b> is about <b>sustainable use and benefit sharing</b>, unlike the Wildlife Act which is about protection.</li>\n          </ul>\n          <div class=\"note exam\">Trap MCQ: \"Which Act is called the umbrella Act?\" → <b>EPA 1986</b>. \"Which Act established CPCB?\" → <b>Water Act 1974</b>. \"Which Act took forest diversion power from states?\" → <b>Forest (Conservation) Act 1980</b>.</div>\n        "},{"title":"Amendments & judgments","html":"\n          <table class=\"tbl\">\n            <thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>Change</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td>1987</td><td>Air Act amended: <b>noise</b> added as an air pollutant.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>1988</td><td>Water Act amended: penalties increased, boards' powers strengthened. Forest Act amended the same year.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>1992</td><td>Forest (Conservation) Act amendment: allowed limited, selected developmental activities with approval.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>1996</td><td><b>T.N. Godavarman judgment</b> (Supreme Court): \"forest\" means any forest in the <b>dictionary sense</b>, whatever its ownership or record. Hugely widened the Forest Act.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>2023</td><td>Forest (Conservation) Amendment Act: renamed and narrowed coverage to recorded forests (coverage clarified to start from October 1980); a current, debated issue.</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n        "}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Which Act created the CPCB and SPCBs?","back":"<b>Water (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act, 1974</b> (Sections 3 and 4)."},{"front":"Which Act is the \"umbrella\" environmental law?","back":"<b>Environment (Protection) Act, 1986</b>."},{"front":"Which disaster triggered the EPA 1986?","back":"<b>Bhopal Gas Tragedy</b>, 2–3 December 1984 (MIC leak)."}],"cardCount":6,"formulaCount":0},{"id":"m6-international","title":"International conventions, protocols and summits","short":"International treaties","blurb":"Stockholm to Paris: every global agreement in the syllabus with its year, place and purpose.","source":"Collected from Lectures W2–W5 (Biodiversity, Chemical hazards, Kyoto, Paris, SDGs, Climate change)","tabs":[{"title":"Quick table","html":"\n          <table class=\"tbl\">\n            <thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>Agreement / Summit</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td><b>1971</b></td><td>Ramsar Convention (Ramsar, Iran)</td><td>Protects <b>wetlands</b> of international importance. India has 75+ Ramsar sites (e.g. Chilika Lake, Keoladeo).</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>1972</b></td><td>Stockholm Conference (UN Conference on the Human Environment)</td><td><b>First major global summit</b> on the environment. Led to UNEP; triggered India's Water, Air and EPA Acts. 5 June became World Environment Day.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>1973</b> (in force 1975)</td><td>CITES</td><td>Controls <b>international trade</b> in endangered species. Secretariat: Geneva. India joined 1976.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>1987</b></td><td>Montreal Protocol</td><td>Phased out <b>CFCs</b> and other ozone-depleting substances. The most successful environmental treaty.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>1987</b></td><td>Brundtland Report (\"Our Common Future\")</td><td>Defined <b>sustainable development</b>: meeting present needs without compromising future generations.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>1988 / 1990</b></td><td>IPCC formed / First Assessment Report</td><td>Scientific basis that triggered the UNFCCC.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>1992</b></td><td>Rio Earth Summit</td><td>Produced <b>UNFCCC</b>, <b>CBD</b> (Convention on Biological Diversity) and <b>Agenda 21</b>.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>1997</b> (adopted 11 Dec; in force 16 Feb 2005)</td><td>Kyoto Protocol (COP3)</td><td><b>First binding emission cuts</b>, for developed (Annex I) countries only. Period 1: 2008–2012. Mechanisms: CDM, JI, emissions trading. India ratified Aug 2002.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>2002</b></td><td>Johannesburg World Summit on Sustainable Development (Rio+10)</td><td>Renewed commitment; Johannesburg Plan of Implementation.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>2009</b></td><td>Copenhagen (COP15)</td><td>Failed to give a binding treaty, but began voluntary pledges.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>2012</b></td><td>Doha Amendment (COP18) &amp; Rio+20</td><td>Kyoto second period to 2020; Rio+20 outcome \"The Future We Want\" started the SDG process.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>2013</b></td><td>Minamata Convention</td><td>Global treaty on <b>mercury</b> (named after Minamata disease, 1956).</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>2015</b> (adopted Dec, COP21; in force 4 Nov 2016)</td><td>Paris Agreement</td><td>196 parties; keep warming <b>well below 2 °C, aim for 1.5 °C</b>; every country submits <b>NDCs</b>; global stocktake every 5 years.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>2015</b></td><td>Sustainable Development Goals</td><td><b>17 SDGs</b> for 2030 (e.g. SDG 6 clean water, SDG 13 climate action).</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>2022 / 2023</td><td>COP27 / COP28 (Dubai)</td><td>COP27 agreed a <b>loss and damage</b> fund, operationalised at COP28; COP28 finished the first global stocktake.</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n          <div class=\"note exam\">Pairs examiners love: <b>Montreal → ozone (CFCs)</b>, <b>Kyoto → binding GHG cuts</b>, <b>Paris → NDCs, 1.5 °C</b>, <b>Minamata → mercury</b>, <b>Ramsar → wetlands</b>, <b>CITES → wildlife trade</b>, <b>CBD → biodiversity</b>.</div>\n        "},{"title":"Kyoto vs Paris","html":"\n          <table class=\"tbl\">\n            <thead><tr><th></th><th>Kyoto Protocol 1997</th><th>Paris Agreement 2015</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td>Who must cut</td><td>Only developed (Annex I) countries</td><td>All countries</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Targets</td><td>Binding, set top-down</td><td>Self-set NDCs, bottom-up</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Goal</td><td>~5% below 1990 levels (2008–12)</td><td>Well below 2 °C, aim 1.5 °C</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>In force</td><td>16 Feb 2005</td><td>4 Nov 2016</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Weakness</td><td>US never ratified; big emitters (China, India) had no targets</td><td>NDCs not legally enforceable; US left 2020, rejoined 2021</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n        "}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Montreal Protocol (1987) deals with?","back":"Phasing out <b>ozone-depleting substances</b> such as CFCs."},{"front":"Kyoto Protocol year and key feature?","back":"<b>1997</b> (COP3): first <b>binding</b> GHG cuts, developed countries only; in force 16 Feb 2005."},{"front":"Paris Agreement goal?","back":"<b>2015</b> (COP21): well below 2 °C, aim for 1.5 °C, through NDCs."}],"cardCount":6,"formulaCount":0},{"id":"m6-events-programmes","title":"Disasters, movements and government programmes","short":"Events & programmes","blurb":"The landmark disasters, case studies and Indian missions that the lectures attach dates to.","source":"Collected from Lectures W1–W5","tabs":[{"title":"Disasters & case studies","html":"\n          <table class=\"tbl\">\n            <thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>Event</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td>1952</td><td>London Great Smog</td><td>Thousands died; led to the UK <b>Clean Air Act 1956</b>.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>1956</td><td>Minamata disease, Japan</td><td><b>Mercury</b> in fish from factory waste; led to the Minamata Convention 2013.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>1967</td><td>Koynanagar earthquake</td><td>Linked to the Koyna dam reservoir (reservoir-induced seismicity).</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>1973–1983</td><td>Silent Valley (Kerala)</td><td>Hydro project halted in 1983 after protests; declared a <b>national park</b>.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>2–3 Dec 1984</td><td><b>Bhopal Gas Tragedy</b></td><td><b>Methyl isocyanate (MIC)</b> leak from Union Carbide plant; worst industrial disaster; triggered the <b>EPA 1986</b>.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>1989</td><td>Exxon Valdez oil spill</td><td>~41 million litres of oil off Alaska.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>2005</td><td>Hurricane Katrina</td><td>Disaster management case study.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>2010</td><td>Deepwater Horizon oil spill; Pakistan floods</td><td>Largest marine oil spill; one of the worst floods.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>2013</td><td>Kedarnath landslide/flood</td><td>Triggered by intense rainfall; Himalayan disaster case.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>2019</td><td>Cyclone Fani</td><td>Odisha's early-warning evacuation success.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>11 Mar 2020</td><td>COVID-19 declared a pandemic</td><td>Biological hazard case study (SARS-CoV-2).</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n        "},{"title":"Indian programmes","html":"\n          <table class=\"tbl\">\n            <thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>Programme</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td>1936</td><td>Jim Corbett National Park</td><td>India's <b>first national park</b> (Uttarakhand).</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>1973</td><td><b>Project Tiger</b></td><td>Tiger conservation via tiger reserves; one of India's biggest successes.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>1992</td><td><b>Project Elephant</b></td><td>Conserve elephants, habitats and corridors.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>1987, 2012</td><td>National Water Policy</td><td>Water planning; 2012 version stresses water as an economic good and efficiency.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>2002</td><td>Bt cotton approved</td><td>India's GM crop case study.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>2008</td><td>NAPCC</td><td>National Action Plan on Climate Change: <b>8 national missions</b> (e.g. Solar Mission).</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>2014 / 2015</td><td>NMCG / <b>Namami Gange</b></td><td>Clean and rejuvenate the Ganga.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>2019</td><td><b>NCAP</b></td><td>National Clean Air Programme: 20–30% cut in PM by 2024 (later 40% by 2026) in non-attainment cities.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>2020</td><td><b>BS-VI</b></td><td>India jumped from BS-IV straight to BS-VI vehicle emission norms.</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n          <div class=\"note\">Important days: <b>22 April</b> Earth Day · <b>5 June</b> World Environment Day · <b>16 September</b> World Ozone Day (Montreal Protocol) · <b>22 March</b> World Water Day · <b>22 May</b> Biodiversity Day.</div>\n        "}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Gas in the Bhopal tragedy?","back":"<b>Methyl isocyanate (MIC)</b>, Union Carbide plant, 1984."},{"front":"Project Tiger began in?","back":"<b>1973</b>."},{"front":"Project Elephant began in?","back":"<b>1992</b>."}],"cardCount":5,"formulaCount":0},{"id":"m6-timeline","title":"Master timeline: the dates that matter","short":"Master timeline","blurb":"The dates worth learning, by topic and in order, with the low-yield ones cut.","source":"Compiled from the other three topics of this section","dates":[["London Great Smog",1952],["Minamata disease identified",1956],["Ramsar Convention (wetlands)",1971],["Stockholm Conference",1972],["Wildlife (Protection) Act",1972],["CITES signed",1973],["Project Tiger",1973],["Water (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act",1974],["Forest (Conservation) Act",1980],["Air (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act",1981],["Silent Valley project halted",1983],["Bhopal Gas Tragedy",1984],["Environment (Protection) Act",1986],["Montreal Protocol",1987],["Brundtland Report",1987],["Air Act amended to add noise",1987],["Rio Earth Summit (UNFCCC, CBD)",1992],["Project Elephant",1992],["Kyoto Protocol adopted",1997],["Noise Pollution (Regulation and Control) Rules",2000],["Biological Diversity Act",2002],["Kyoto Protocol entered into force",2005],["NAPCC (8 missions)",2008],["National Green Tribunal set up",2010],["Minamata Convention",2013],["Paris Agreement adopted",2015],["SDGs adopted",2015],["Solid Waste Management Rules",2016],["NCAP",2019]],"tabs":[{"title":"Must know (in order)","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">The <b>19 years to learn first</b>, oldest to newest. Short on time? Learn this tab. The topic tabs after it add the second-tier dates, and the two <b>All years</b> tabs at the end list every date in one combined timeline.</p>\n          <table class=\"tbl\">\n            <thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>What</th><th>Topic</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td><b>1956</b></td><td>Minamata disease (mercury)</td><td>Disaster</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>1971</b></td><td>Ramsar Convention (wetlands)</td><td>Global</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>1972</b></td><td><b>Stockholm Conference</b> + <b>Wildlife (Protection) Act</b></td><td>Global / India law</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>1973</b></td><td>CITES (wildlife trade) + <b>Project Tiger</b></td><td>Global / Programme</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>1974</b></td><td><b>Water Act</b> &rarr; CPCB &amp; SPCBs</td><td>India law</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>1980</b></td><td><b>Forest (Conservation) Act</b></td><td>India law</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>1981</b></td><td><b>Air Act</b></td><td>India law</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>1984</b></td><td><b>Bhopal Gas Tragedy</b> (MIC)</td><td>Disaster</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>1986</b></td><td><b>Environment (Protection) Act</b>, the umbrella law</td><td>India law</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>1987</b></td><td><b>Montreal Protocol</b> (ozone) + Brundtland Report + noise added to Air Act</td><td>Global / India law</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>1992</b></td><td><b>Rio Earth Summit</b> (UNFCCC, CBD, Agenda 21) + Project Elephant</td><td>Global / Programme</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>1997</b></td><td><b>Kyoto Protocol</b> (COP3)</td><td>Climate</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>2002</b></td><td><b>Biological Diversity Act</b></td><td>India law</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>2008</b></td><td>NAPCC (8 missions)</td><td>Programme</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>2010</b></td><td><b>National Green Tribunal</b></td><td>India law</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>2013</b></td><td><b>Minamata Convention</b> (mercury)</td><td>Global</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>2015</b></td><td><b>Paris Agreement</b> (COP21) + <b>17 SDGs</b></td><td>Climate / Global</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>2016</b></td><td><b>SWM Rules</b> (segregation at source)</td><td>India law</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>2019</b></td><td><b>NCAP</b> (clean air)</td><td>Programme</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n        "},{"title":"How to remember","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">Don't learn 30 years one by one. Learn a few <b>anchor years</b>, then hang everything else on them as \"+2\", \"+10\" or \"same year\".</p>\n          <h3>1. The five Indian Acts: \"Wild Water, Forest Air, Everything\"</h3>\n          <table class=\"tbl\">\n            <thead><tr><th>Say</th><th>Act</th><th>Year</th><th>Hook</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td><b>Wild</b></td><td>Wildlife</td><td>1972</td><td>Same year as <b>Stockholm</b>: India acted the year the world met.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Water</b></td><td>Water</td><td>1974</td><td>Stockholm <b>+2</b>. Water came first, so it created the <b>CPCB</b>.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Forest</b></td><td>Forest</td><td>1980</td><td>Start of the 80s. Forests first...</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Air</b></td><td>Air</td><td>1981</td><td>...then the air they clean, <b>one year later</b>.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Everything</b></td><td>EPA (umbrella)</td><td>1986</td><td>Bhopal 1984 <b>+2</b>. \"Everything\" = the umbrella law.</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n          <div class=\"note exam\">Pattern: <b>72, 74</b> (two years apart) then <b>80, 81</b> (back to back) then <b>86</b>. Acts are 70s–80s; <b>Rules</b> (Noise 2000, SWM 2016) come in the 2000s.</div>\n          <h3>2. The \"+2\" and \"+10\" chains</h3>\n          <ul>\n            <li><b>Stockholm 1972 → Water Act 1974</b> (+2).</li>\n            <li><b>Bhopal 1984 → EPA 1986</b> (+2) <b>→ noise added to Air Act 1987</b> (+1).</li>\n            <li><b>Montreal 1987 → Kyoto 1997</b> (+10): ozone, then climate.</li>\n            <li><b>CBD 1992 → Biological Diversity Act 2002</b> (+10): India's law for the Rio treaty.</li>\n            <li><b>Kyoto adopted 1997 → in force 2005</b> (8 years); <b>Paris adopted 2015 → in force 2016</b> (just 1 year).</li>\n          </ul>\n          <h3>3. \"Same year\" pairs</h3>\n          <ul>\n            <li><b>1956</b>: Minamata disease <b>and</b> the UK Clean Air Act.</li>\n            <li><b>1972</b>: Stockholm <b>and</b> the Wildlife Act.</li>\n            <li><b>1987</b>: Montreal Protocol, Brundtland Report, Air Act noise amendment.</li>\n            <li><b>1992</b>: Rio (UNFCCC + CBD + Agenda 21) <b>and</b> Project Elephant.</li>\n            <li><b>2015</b>: Paris Agreement <b>and</b> the 17 SDGs.</li>\n          </ul>\n          <h3>4. Small hooks</h3>\n          <ul>\n            <li><b>Project Tiger 1973</b> = Wildlife Act <b>+1</b>.</li>\n            <li><b>Minamata Convention 2013</b> is named after the 1956 mercury disease; both are about <b>mercury</b>.</li>\n            <li><b>NGT 2010</b>: round number, the \"green court of the new decade\".</li>\n            <li><b>BS-VI 2020</b>: \"<b>six</b> in twenty-twenty\" (India skipped BS-V).</li>\n            <li><b>Ramsar 1971</b> is the <b>oldest</b> treaty in the syllabus: \"wetlands first\".</li>\n          </ul>\n          <div class=\"note\">Then use the <b>date drill</b> at the top of the Laws, Acts &amp; dates page until you can answer 20 in a row.</div>\n        "},{"title":"Indian laws","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">Oldest to newest. <b>&#9733; bold</b> = must know; plain = good to know.</p>\n          <table class=\"tbl\">\n            <thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>What</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td><b>1972</b></td><td>&#9733; Wildlife (Protection) Act: Schedules, protects the <b>inhabitants</b></td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>1974</b></td><td>&#9733; Water Act: created the <b>CPCB &amp; SPCBs</b></td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>1980</b></td><td>&#9733; Forest (Conservation) Act: Centre approval, protects the <b>habitat</b></td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>1981</b></td><td>&#9733; Air Act: same boards, now for air</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>1986</b></td><td>&#9733; Environment (Protection) Act: <b>umbrella law</b>, after Bhopal</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>1987</b></td><td>&#9733; Air Act amended: <b>noise</b> added</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>1996</td><td>Godavarman judgment: \"forest\" in the dictionary sense</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>2000</td><td>Noise Pollution Rules (made under the EPA)</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>2002</b></td><td>&#9733; Biological Diversity Act: India's law for the CBD</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>2010</b></td><td>&#9733; National Green Tribunal Act</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>2016</b></td><td>&#9733; Solid Waste Management Rules: segregation at source</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>2023</td><td>Forest (Conservation) Amendment Act</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n        "},{"title":"Global treaties","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">Oldest to newest. <b>&#9733; bold</b> = must know; plain = good to know.</p>\n          <table class=\"tbl\">\n            <thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>What</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td><b>1971</b></td><td>&#9733; Ramsar Convention: wetlands</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>1972</b></td><td>&#9733; Stockholm Conference: first global summit, UNEP, 5 June</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>1973</b></td><td>&#9733; CITES: trade in endangered species</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>1987</b></td><td>&#9733; Montreal Protocol: ozone / CFCs</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>1987</b></td><td>&#9733; Brundtland Report: defined sustainable development</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>1992</b></td><td>&#9733; Rio Earth Summit: UNFCCC, CBD, Agenda 21</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>1997</b></td><td>&#9733; Kyoto Protocol adopted: binding cuts, developed countries only</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>2005</td><td>Kyoto enters into force (16 Feb)</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>2013</b></td><td>&#9733; Minamata Convention: mercury</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>2015</b></td><td>&#9733; Paris Agreement (COP21): 1.5 &deg;C, NDCs</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>2015</b></td><td>&#9733; 17 SDGs for 2030</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>2016</td><td>Paris enters into force (4 Nov)</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n        "},{"title":"Disasters","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">Oldest to newest. <b>&#9733; bold</b> = must know; plain = good to know.</p>\n          <table class=\"tbl\">\n            <thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>What</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td>1952</td><td>London Great Smog &rarr; UK Clean Air Act 1956</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>1956</b></td><td>&#9733; Minamata disease: mercury in fish</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>1983</td><td>Silent Valley hydro project halted</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>1984</b></td><td>&#9733; Bhopal Gas Tragedy: MIC, Union Carbide &rarr; EPA 1986</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n        "},{"title":"Programmes & days","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">Oldest to newest. <b>&#9733; bold</b> = must know; plain = good to know.</p>\n          <table class=\"tbl\">\n            <thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>What</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td><b>1936</b></td><td>&#9733; Jim Corbett: India's first national park</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>1973</b></td><td>&#9733; Project Tiger</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>1992</b></td><td>&#9733; Project Elephant</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>2008</b></td><td>&#9733; NAPCC: 8 national missions</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>2015</td><td>Namami Gange</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>2019</b></td><td>&#9733; NCAP: cut PM in non-attainment cities</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>2020</td><td>BS-VI norms (skipped BS-V)</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n          <div class=\"note\">Days, in calendar order: <b>22 Mar</b> Water &middot; <b>22 Apr</b> Earth &middot; <b>22 May</b> Biodiversity (three 22nds) &middot; <b>5 Jun</b> Environment &middot; <b>16 Sep</b> Ozone.</div>\n          <h3>Skipped on purpose</h3>\n          <p>These dates appear in the lectures but are rarely asked, so they are left off this timeline and the date drill: Koyna 1967, Exxon Valdez 1989, Katrina 2005, Deepwater Horizon and Pakistan floods 2010, Kedarnath 2013, Fani 2019, COVID-19 2020, IPCC 1988, Johannesburg 2002, Copenhagen 2009, Doha / Rio+20 2012, COP27&ndash;28, the 1988 and 1992 Act amendments, National Water Policy, Bt cotton. Know what the <b>event</b> was; don't learn its year.</p>\n        "},{"title":"All years: 1936 – 1991","html":"\n          <table class=\"tbl\">\n            <thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>What</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td>1936</td><td>Jim Corbett, India's first national park</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>1952 / 1956</td><td>London Smog → UK Clean Air Act 1956; Minamata disease 1956</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>1971</td><td>Ramsar Convention (wetlands)</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>1972</td><td><b>Stockholm Conference</b>; <b>Wildlife (Protection) Act</b></td></tr>\n              <tr><td>1973</td><td>CITES signed; <b>Project Tiger</b></td></tr>\n              <tr><td>1974</td><td><b>Water Act</b> → CPCB &amp; SPCBs</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>1980</td><td><b>Forest (Conservation) Act</b></td></tr>\n              <tr><td>1981</td><td><b>Air Act</b></td></tr>\n              <tr><td>1983</td><td>Silent Valley project halted</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>1984</td><td><b>Bhopal Gas Tragedy</b> (MIC)</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>1986</td><td><b>Environment (Protection) Act</b>, the umbrella law</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>1987</td><td><b>Montreal Protocol</b>; Brundtland Report; Air Act amended (noise); National Water Policy</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>1988</td><td>Water Act &amp; Forest Act amended; IPCC formed</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n        "},{"title":"All years: 1992 – today","html":"\n          <table class=\"tbl\">\n            <thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>What</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td>1992</td><td><b>Rio Earth Summit</b> (UNFCCC, CBD, Agenda 21); Project Elephant; Forest Act amendment</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>1996</td><td>Godavarman judgment</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>1997</td><td><b>Kyoto Protocol</b> (COP3)</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>2000</td><td>Noise Pollution Rules</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>2002</td><td><b>Biological Diversity Act</b>; Johannesburg summit; Bt cotton approved; India ratifies Kyoto</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>2005</td><td>Kyoto enters into force (16 Feb)</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>2008</td><td>NAPCC (8 missions); Kyoto period 1 begins (2008–12)</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>2009</td><td>Copenhagen COP15</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>2010</td><td><b>National Green Tribunal</b></td></tr>\n              <tr><td>2012</td><td>Doha Amendment; Rio+20; National Water Policy 2012</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>2013</td><td><b>Minamata Convention</b> (mercury)</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>2014–15</td><td>Namami Gange</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>2015</td><td><b>Paris Agreement</b> (COP21); <b>17 SDGs</b></td></tr>\n              <tr><td>2016</td><td>Paris in force (4 Nov); <b>SWM Rules</b></td></tr>\n              <tr><td>2019</td><td><b>NCAP</b></td></tr>\n              <tr><td>2020</td><td>BS-VI; COVID-19 pandemic declared</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>2022–23</td><td>COP27 loss &amp; damage fund; COP28 global stocktake; Forest Act 2023 amendment</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n        "}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Order the five big Indian Acts.","back":"Wildlife 1972 → Water 1974 → Forest 1980 → Air 1981 → EPA 1986."},{"front":"Order the climate treaties.","back":"UNFCCC 1992 → Kyoto 1997 → Paris 2015."},{"front":"Which happened in 1987?","back":"Montreal Protocol, Brundtland Report, Air Act amendment (noise)."}],"cardCount":4,"formulaCount":0},{"id":"m6-articles","title":"Articles: the Constitution and the Paris Agreement","short":"Articles","blurb":"Every Article number in the syllabus, how to remember it, and one table with all of them.","source":"Compiled from the Need for env laws, Water Act, Forest & Wildlife and Paris Agreement topics","articles":[["Clean environment as a fundamental right (right to life)","21","c"],["Parliament legislates on a state subject at states' request (basis of the Water Act)","252","c"],["The State's duty to protect the environment, forests and wildlife","48A","c"],["Every citizen's fundamental duty to protect the environment","51A(g)","c"],["Paris: hold warming well below 2°C, pursue 1.5°C","2","p"],["Paris: peak emissions, then net zero in the second half of the century","4","p"],["Paris: adaptation, on equal footing with mitigation","7","p"],["Paris: finance, USD 100 billion a year","9","p"]],"tabs":[{"title":"How to remember","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">There are only <b>eight</b> Article numbers: four from India's <b>Constitution</b> and four from the <b>Paris Agreement</b>. First ask \"India or Paris?\", then use the hooks.</p>\n          <h3>1. Constitution: \"Life, Law, State, Citizen\"</h3>\n          <table class=\"tbl\">\n            <thead><tr><th>Say</th><th>Article</th><th>What</th><th>Hook</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td><b>Life</b></td><td>21</td><td>Right to life includes a clean, healthy environment (Supreme Court)</td><td>\"<b>21</b>, the age you start <b>living</b> your own life\": the <b>right to life</b>.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Law</b></td><td>252</td><td>Parliament makes a law on a state subject (water) when states ask</td><td><b>2-5-2</b> reads the same both ways: <b>States</b> ask the <b>Centre</b>, the Centre makes the law <b>for the States</b>. The Water Act 1974 was made this way.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>State</b></td><td>48A</td><td>Directive Principle: the State shall protect the environment, forests and wildlife</td><td><b>A</b> for <b>Administration</b> (the State). Comes <b>before</b> 51A: the State's duty first.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Citizen</b></td><td>51A(g)</td><td>Fundamental Duty of every citizen to protect nature and have compassion for living creatures</td><td><b>g</b> for <b>green</b>. 51A is the list of <b>citizens'</b> duties.</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n          <div class=\"note exam\">Trap: <b>Article 21</b> is the <b>right</b>, <b>48A</b> the <b>State's duty</b>, <b>51A(g)</b> the <b>citizen's duty</b>. Articles <b>14, 19, 32</b> are equality, freedoms and the right to move the Supreme Court; they appear only as wrong options.</div>\n          <h3>2. Paris Agreement: \"2-4-7-9 = Goal, Zero, Adapt, Money\"</h3>\n          <ul>\n            <li><b>Article 2, Goal</b>: <b>2</b>°C. The article number <b>is</b> the temperature.</li>\n            <li><b>Article 4, Zero</b>: \"<b>four</b> → <b>for</b> net zero\". Mitigation and NDCs live here.</li>\n            <li><b>Article 7, Adapt</b>: <b>seven</b> days a week you adapt to the weather.</li>\n            <li><b>Article 9, Money</b>: <b>9</b> looks like a money bag; USD <b>100 billion</b> via the Green Climate Fund.</li>\n          </ul>\n          <div class=\"note\">Even numbers 2, 4 = <b>cutting warming</b> (goal and net zero). Odd numbers 7, 9 = <b>living with it and paying for it</b>.</div>\n          <div class=\"note\">Then use the <b>Article drill</b> at the top of the Laws, Acts &amp; dates page until you can answer 20 in a row.</div>\n        "},{"title":"All articles","html":"\n          <table class=\"tbl\">\n            <thead><tr><th>Article</th><th>Source</th><th>What</th><th>Where it comes up</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td><b>21</b></td><td>Constitution</td><td>Right to life; includes a clean, healthy environment</td><td>Need for environmental laws</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>48A</b></td><td>Constitution (Directive Principle)</td><td>State shall protect and improve the environment, forests and wildlife</td><td>Forest Act, Wildlife Act</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>51A(g)</b></td><td>Constitution (Fundamental Duty)</td><td>Every citizen shall protect the natural environment</td><td>Forest Act, Wildlife Act</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>252</b></td><td>Constitution</td><td>Parliament legislates on a state subject at states' request</td><td>Water Act 1974</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>2</b></td><td>Paris Agreement</td><td>Well below 2°C, pursuing 1.5°C</td><td>Paris basics</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>4</b></td><td>Paris Agreement</td><td>Peak emissions, net zero in the second half of the century; NDCs</td><td>Paris pillars</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>7</b></td><td>Paris Agreement</td><td>Adaptation, equal footing with mitigation</td><td>Paris pillars</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>9</b></td><td>Paris Agreement</td><td>Finance: USD 100 billion a year, Green Climate Fund</td><td>Paris pillars</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n        "}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Article for the right to a clean environment?","back":"<b>Article 21</b> (right to life)."},{"front":"State's duty vs citizen's duty?","back":"State: <b>Article 48A</b>. Citizen: <b>Article 51A(g)</b>."},{"front":"Constitutional basis of the Water Act?","back":"<b>Article 252</b>."}],"cardCount":4,"formulaCount":0},{"id":"m6-sections","title":"Sections: EPA, Air Act, Water Act and Forest Act","short":"Sections","blurb":"Every Section number in the syllabus, a hook for each Act, and one table with all of them.","source":"Compiled from the EPA 1986, Air Act, Water Act and Forest Act topics (Week 4, lectures 22-24)","sections":[["EPA 1986: the Centre's powers, the \"master power switch\"","3","e"],["EPA 1986: numerical standards","6","e"],["EPA 1986: no discharge beyond the standards","7","e"],["EPA 1986: hazardous substances (the Bhopal response)","8","e"],["EPA 1986: entry, inspection and samples","10","e"],["Air Act 1981: definition of \"air pollutant\"","2(a)","a"],["Air Act 1981: Air Pollution Control Areas","19","a"],["Air Act 1981: consent of the State Board","21","a"],["Air Act 1981: entry, and samples from chimneys","24","a"],["Air Act 1981: emergency closure without a court","31A","a"],["Water Act 1974: sets up the CPCB","3","w"],["Water Act 1974: sets up the SPCBs","4","w"],["Water Act 1974: Joint Board for two or more states","13","w"],["Water Act 1974: Boards give binding directions","18","w"],["Water Act 1974: effluent samples","21","w"],["Water Act 1974: entry and inspection","23","w"],["Water Act 1974: absolute ban on polluting a stream or well","24","w"],["Water Act 1974: consent for new outlets","25","w"],["Water Act 1974: existing discharges must get consent by a deadline","26","w"],["Water Act 1974: revocation of consent","27","w"],["Water Act 1974: emergency measures","32","w"],["Water Act 1974: Boards apply to courts to stop apprehended pollution","33","w"],["Water Act 1974: appeal to the NGT","33B","w"],["Water Act 1974: obstructing officers (3 months or ₹10,000)","42","w"],["Water Act 1974: the most severe penalty, for breaking Section 24","43","w"],["Water Act 1974: continuing violation, ₹5,000 per day","45","w"],["Water Act 1974: companies (directors, managers, engineers liable)","46","w"],["Water Act 1974: Government departments (Head of Department liable)","47","w"],["Forest (Conservation) Act 1980: no non-forest use of forest land without prior Central approval","2","f"]],"tabs":[{"title":"How to remember","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">First ask <b>which Act</b>, then use that Act's hook. The same number means different things in different Acts (see the traps).</p>\n          <h3>1. EPA 1986: \"The Centre Powers up, sets a Limit, draws the Line, bans the Gas, sends an Inspector\"</h3>\n          <table class=\"tbl\">\n            <thead><tr><th>Say</th><th>Section</th><th>What</th><th>Hook</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td><b>Power</b></td><td>3</td><td>Central powers (the \"master power switch\")</td><td>A <b>3</b>-pin plug: the master power switch.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Limit</b></td><td>6</td><td>Numerical standards</td><td><b>6</b> <b>s</b>ets the <b>s</b>tandards: the number limit.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Line</b></td><td>7</td><td>No discharge beyond standards</td><td>6 sets the limit, the <b>next</b> one (7) says don't cross it.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Gas</b></td><td>8</td><td>Hazardous substances (Bhopal response)</td><td><b>8</b> looks like two gas cylinders stacked: Bhopal gas.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Inspector</b></td><td>10</td><td>Entry, inspection, samples</td><td><b>1</b> inspector + <b>0</b> his magnifying glass.</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n          <h3>2. Air Act 1981: \"Define, Zone, Consent, Chimney, Close\"</h3>\n          <ul>\n            <li><b>2(a), Define</b>: Section 2 is the definitions; <b>a</b> for <b>air</b> pollutant (solid, liquid or gas at injurious concentration).</li>\n            <li><b>19, Zone</b>: like <b>COVID-19</b> zones, the State marks out <b>Air Pollution Control Areas</b>.</li>\n            <li><b>21, Consent</b>: at <b>21</b> you are an adult who signs for yourself: get the <b>State Board's consent</b>.</li>\n            <li><b>24, Chimney</b>: <b>24</b>-hour smoke from a chimney: entry and <b>samples from chimneys</b>.</li>\n            <li><b>31A, Close</b>: <b>A</b> for <b>at once</b>: emergency closure <b>without a court</b>.</li>\n          </ul>\n          <h3>3. Water Act 1974: five groups</h3>\n          <table class=\"tbl\">\n            <thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Sections</th><th>Hook</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td><b>Boards</b></td><td>3 CPCB, 4 SPCB, 13 Joint Board, 18 directions</td><td>\"<b>Centre 3, State 4</b>\": the Centre comes first. 13 = <b>1</b> river, <b>3</b>+ states sharing it. 18 = adult: Boards give <b>orders</b>.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Checks</b></td><td>21 samples, 23 entry</td><td>\"<b>21 Sip, 23 Step in</b>\": take a sample, then walk in.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Discharge</b></td><td>24 ban, 25 new, 26 old, 27 gone</td><td>\"<b>Ban, New, Old, Gone</b>\": count up from 24.</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Help</b></td><td>28/29 State Govt, 32 emergency, 33 courts, 33B NGT</td><td>\"<b>State, Siren, Court, Green Bench</b>\": 33<b>B</b> = <b>B</b>ench (the NGT, set up 2010).</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Penalties</b></td><td>41-47</td><td>The <b>40s</b> punish. 42 blocked officer (3 months or ₹10,000); <b>43</b> the worst, for breaking 24; 4<b>5</b> = ₹<b>5</b>,000 a day; 46 <b>C</b>ompanies before 47 <b>G</b>overnment (C before G).</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n          <h3>4. Forest Act 1980: \"Section 2 = two keys\"</h3>\n          <p>Forest land needs <b>two keys</b> before non-forest use: the State's and the <b>Centre's prior approval</b>. Section 2 is the heart of the Act.</p>\n          <h3>5. Wildlife Act 1972: Schedules, not Sections</h3>\n          <p>Four Schedules since 2022. <b>Schedule I</b> = highest protection, <b>3 to 7 years</b> plus fine. Its animals: \"<b>The Elephant and Rhino Saw a Bustard</b>\": tiger, elephant, rhino, snow leopard, great Indian bustard.</p>\n          <div class=\"note exam\">Traps: <b>21</b> is <b>consent</b> in the Air Act but <b>samples</b> in the Water Act. <b>24</b> is <b>chimney samples</b> in the Air Act but the <b>absolute ban</b> in the Water Act. <b>3</b> is the Centre's powers in the EPA and the <b>CPCB</b> in the Water Act. Emergencies: Air <b>31A</b> closes at once, Water <b>32</b> takes emergency measures.</div>\n          <div class=\"note\">Then use the <b>Section drill</b> at the top of the Laws, Acts &amp; dates page until you can answer 20 in a row.</div>\n        "},{"title":"All sections","html":"\n          <table class=\"tbl\">\n            <thead><tr><th>Act</th><th>Section</th><th>What</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td>EPA 1986</td><td><b>3</b></td><td>Central powers (\"master power switch\")</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>EPA 1986</td><td><b>6</b></td><td>Numerical standards</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>EPA 1986</td><td><b>7</b></td><td>No discharge beyond standards</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>EPA 1986</td><td><b>8</b></td><td>Hazardous substances (Bhopal response)</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>EPA 1986</td><td><b>10</b></td><td>Entry, inspection, samples</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Air Act 1981</td><td><b>2(a)</b></td><td>Air pollutant = solid, liquid or gaseous substance at injurious concentration</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Air Act 1981</td><td><b>19</b></td><td>Air Pollution Control Areas</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Air Act 1981</td><td><b>21</b></td><td>Consent of State Board</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Air Act 1981</td><td><b>24</b></td><td>Entry; samples from chimneys</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Air Act 1981</td><td><b>31A</b></td><td>Emergency closure without court</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Water Act 1974</td><td><b>3</b></td><td>CPCB (national): advises Centre, coordinates SPCBs, national standards</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Water Act 1974</td><td><b>4</b></td><td>SPCB (state): consent, monitoring, labs, prosecution</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Water Act 1974</td><td><b>13</b></td><td>Joint Board: two or more states (inter-state rivers)</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Water Act 1974</td><td><b>18</b></td><td>Boards give binding directions</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Water Act 1974</td><td><b>21</b></td><td>Effluent samples</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Water Act 1974</td><td><b>23</b></td><td>Entry and inspection</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Water Act 1974</td><td><b>24</b></td><td>Absolute prohibition: no polluting matter into a stream or well</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Water Act 1974</td><td><b>25</b></td><td>Consent for new outlets</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Water Act 1974</td><td><b>26</b></td><td>Existing discharges: consent by deadline</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Water Act 1974</td><td><b>27</b></td><td>Revocation of consent</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Water Act 1974</td><td><b>28 / 29</b></td><td>Appeal to / revision by State Govt</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Water Act 1974</td><td><b>32</b></td><td>Emergency measures</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Water Act 1974</td><td><b>33</b></td><td>Boards apply to courts to stop apprehended pollution</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Water Act 1974</td><td><b>33B</b></td><td>Appeal to NGT (set up 2010)</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Water Act 1974</td><td><b>41, 41A, 44</b></td><td>Board directions, court orders, no consent: 1.5-6 years + fine</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Water Act 1974</td><td><b>42</b></td><td>Obstructing officers: up to 3 months or ₹10,000</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Water Act 1974</td><td><b>43</b></td><td>Breaking Section 24: 1.5-6 years (most severe)</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Water Act 1974</td><td><b>45</b></td><td>Continuing violation: ₹5,000 per day</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Water Act 1974</td><td><b>46</b></td><td>Companies: directors, managers, engineers liable</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Water Act 1974</td><td><b>47</b></td><td>Govt departments: Head of Department liable</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Forest (Conservation) Act 1980</td><td><b>2</b></td><td>No non-forest use or de-reserving without prior Central approval</td></tr>\n              <tr><td>Wildlife (Protection) Act 1972</td><td><b>Schedule I</b></td><td>Highest protection: tiger, elephant, rhino, snow leopard, great Indian bustard; 3 to 7 years + fine</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n        "}],"flashcards":[{"front":"EPA Sections 3, 6, 7, 8, 10?","back":"Powers, standards, no discharge beyond standards, hazardous substances, inspection."},{"front":"Air Act Sections 19, 21, 24, 31A?","back":"Control areas, consent, chimney samples, emergency closure."},{"front":"Section 21 in the Air Act vs the Water Act?","back":"Air: <b>consent</b>. Water: <b>effluent samples</b>."}],"cardCount":5,"formulaCount":0},{"id":"m6-names","title":"Names, full forms, places and organisms","short":"Names & full forms","blurb":"Every person, abbreviation, place, model and organism the notes name, for \"expand X\", \"who proposed Y\" and \"where is Z\" questions.","source":"Compiled from all weeks of the notes","tabs":[{"title":"Exam summary","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">The must-know names and full forms; the later tabs hold the full lists.</p>\n          <h3>Full forms</h3>\n          <table class=\"tbl\">\n            <thead><tr><th>Short</th><th>Full form</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td><b>AQI</b></td><td>Air Quality Index (0–500)</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>BOD</b></td><td>Biochemical Oxygen Demand</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>CAMPA</b></td><td>Compensatory Afforestation Fund (Management and Planning Authority)</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>CBDR-RC</b></td><td>Common But Differentiated Responsibilities and Respective Capabilities</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>CDM</b></td><td>Clean Development Mechanism (Kyoto)</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>CITES</b></td><td>Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>CPCB / SPCB</b></td><td>Central / State Pollution Control Board</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>EIA / EMP</b></td><td>Environmental Impact Assessment / Environmental Management Plan</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>EPR</b></td><td>Extended Producer Responsibility</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>GEAC</b></td><td>Genetic Engineering Appraisal Committee (GM crops)</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>GPP / NPP</b></td><td>Gross / Net Primary Productivity</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>GWP</b></td><td>Global Warming Potential</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>IPCC</b></td><td>Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>IWRM</b></td><td>Integrated Water Resources Management</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>LCA</b></td><td>Life Cycle Assessment</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>MoEFCC</b></td><td>Ministry of Environment, Forest and Climate Change</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>NAPCC</b></td><td>National Action Plan on Climate Change (2008)</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>NDC</b></td><td>Nationally Determined Contribution (Paris)</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>NGT</b></td><td>National Green Tribunal (2010)</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>SEIAA / SEAC</b></td><td>State Level EIA Authority / State Level Expert Appraisal Committee</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>UNFCCC</b></td><td>United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change (1992)</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>ZLD</b></td><td>Zero Liquid Discharge</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n          <h3>People</h3>\n          <table class=\"tbl\">\n            <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Known for</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td><b>Charles Elton</b></td><td>Ecological pyramids (Eltonian pyramid), 1927</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>F.E. Clements</b> (notes: Clemens)</td><td>Monoclimax theory</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Tansley</b></td><td>Polyclimax theory</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Lindemann</b></td><td>10% law of energy transfer</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n          <h3>Places, models and organisms</h3>\n          <table class=\"tbl\">\n            <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Why it is asked</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td><b>Bhadla Solar Park</b></td><td>Largest solar park in the world (Rajasthan)</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Chilika Lake, Keoladeo NP</b></td><td>Ramsar sites</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Kaziranga NP</b> (Assam)</td><td>One-horned rhinoceros</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Gir NP</b> (Gujarat)</td><td>Asiatic lions: under 20 once, over 600 now</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Ranipet</b> (Tamil Nadu)</td><td>Chromium groundwater pollution from tannery waste</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Kalundborg</b> (Denmark)</td><td>Industrial symbiosis</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Yellowstone</b> (1995)</td><td>Wolves reintroduced: trophic cascade</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Sardar Sarovar Dam</b></td><td>Displaced about 320,000 people</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>India's hotspots</b></td><td>Himalayas, Western Ghats, Indo-Burma, Sundaland (4 of 36)</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Leopold matrix</b></td><td>EIA matrix method</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>AERMOD / QUAL2K, SWAT</b></td><td>Air dispersion model / water quality models in EIA</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>LSTM</b></td><td>Long Short-Term Memory AI model; forecasts air quality</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Bacillus thuringiensis</b></td><td>Source of the Bt gene in Bt cotton</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Rhizobium / Nitrosomonas, Nitrobacter</b></td><td>N fixation / nitrification</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n        "},{"title":"Full forms: organisations","html":"\n          <table class=\"tbl\">\n            <thead><tr><th>Short</th><th>Full form</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td><b>BEE</b></td><td>Bureau of Energy Efficiency</td><td>Star labels for appliances</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>BIS</b></td><td>Bureau of Indian Standards</td><td>Standards for food-contact plastics</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>BMCs</b></td><td>Biodiversity Management Committees</td><td>Local bodies under the Biological Diversity Act; fight biopiracy</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>CAMPA</b></td><td>Compensatory Afforestation Fund (Management and Planning Authority)</td><td>Receives money for forest land diverted, funds afforestation</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>CBD</b></td><td>Convention on Biological Diversity</td><td>1992 treaty: conservation, sustainable use, fair benefit sharing</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>CITES</b></td><td>Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of Wild Fauna and Flora</td><td>Controls wildlife trade; signed 1973, India joined 1976</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>CPCB</b></td><td>Central Pollution Control Board</td><td>Apex pollution authority; sets NAAQS</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>EAC</b></td><td>Expert Appraisal Committee</td><td>Appraises Category A projects (national)</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>ENVIS</b></td><td>Environmental Information System</td><td>Network of environmental databases across India</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>FEMA</b></td><td>Federal Emergency Management Agency (USA)</td><td>Immediate relief and long-term support</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>FSSAI</b></td><td>Food Safety and Standards Authority of India</td><td>Approves food additives and colours</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>GEAC</b></td><td>Genetic Engineering Appraisal Committee</td><td>Clears GM crops under MoEFCC</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>GOARN</b></td><td>Global Outbreak Alert and Response Network (WHO)</td><td>Outbreak surveillance</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>IAA</b></td><td>Impact Assessment Agency</td><td>Appraises Category A projects with EAC</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>IARC</b></td><td>International Agency for Research on Cancer</td><td>Classifies carcinogens (formaldehyde: Group 1)</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>IDSP</b></td><td>Integrated Disease Surveillance Programme (India)</td><td>Disease surveillance</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>IEA</b></td><td>International Energy Agency</td><td>Publishes emission factors</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>IPCC</b></td><td>Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change</td><td>Reviews research, no original work; 1st report 1990, AR6 2021</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>ISA</b></td><td>International Solar Alliance</td><td>Co-founded by India and France; mobilise over $1 trillion</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>MNRE</b></td><td>Ministry of New and Renewable Energy</td><td>Nodal ministry for wind policy</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>MoEFCC</b></td><td>Ministry of Environment, Forest and Climate Change</td><td>Regulates environment, GM crops (via GEAC)</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>NASA / NOAA</b></td><td>National Aeronautics and Space Administration / National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration</td><td>Confirm warming records</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>NBPGR</b></td><td>National Bureau of Plant Genetic Resources</td><td>New Delhi; conserves plant genetic resources</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>NDMA</b></td><td>National Disaster Management Authority</td><td>Disaster preparedness policy (India)</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>NGT</b></td><td>National Green Tribunal</td><td>Fast-track environmental court, 2010</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>NIWE</b></td><td>National Institute of Wind Energy</td><td>Chennai</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>NMCG</b></td><td>National Mission for Clean Ganga</td><td>Ganga clean-up, 2014 (Namami Gange)</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>SEAC</b></td><td>State Level Expert Appraisal Committee</td><td>Appraises Category B projects</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>SEIAA</b></td><td>State Level EIA Authority</td><td>Clears Category B projects</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>SPCB</b></td><td>State Pollution Control Board</td><td>Implements the law in each state</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>UNDRR</b></td><td>UN Office for Disaster Risk Reduction</td><td>Risk reduction</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>UNESCO</b></td><td>UN Educational, Scientific and Cultural Organization</td><td>MAB Programme, World Heritage Sites</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>UNFCCC</b></td><td>United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change</td><td>1992 parent climate treaty; no binding targets</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>UNOCHA</b></td><td>UN Office for the Coordination of Humanitarian Affairs</td><td>Coordinates emergency response</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>WHO</b></td><td>World Health Organization</td><td>Health guidelines, IHR, malaria vaccines</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>WCCB</b></td><td>Wildlife Crime Control Bureau</td><td>Anti-poaching, anti-trafficking</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n        "},{"title":"Full forms: terms","html":"\n          <table class=\"tbl\">\n            <thead><tr><th>Short</th><th>Full form</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td><b>ACTs</b></td><td>Artemisinin-based Combination Therapies</td><td>First-line malaria treatment</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>AQI</b></td><td>Air Quality Index</td><td>0–500 scale from 5–8 pollutants</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>BOD</b></td><td>Biochemical Oxygen Demand</td><td>Organic load of water; secondary treatment removes 85–95%</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>BPA</b></td><td>Bisphenol A</td><td>Plastic chemical; endocrine disruptor</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>BS-VI</b></td><td>Bharat Stage VI</td><td>Vehicle emission norms</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>CBDR-RC</b></td><td>Common But Differentiated Responsibilities and Respective Capabilities</td><td>All share duty; rich nations lead</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>CCS / CCU</b></td><td>Carbon Capture and Storage / Utilisation</td><td>Trap CO₂ and store or use it</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>CDM</b></td><td>Clean Development Mechanism</td><td>Annex I invests in developing country, earns CERs</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>CERs</b></td><td>Certified Emission Reductions</td><td>Carbon credits from CDM</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>CETP / ETP / STP</b></td><td>Common Effluent / Effluent / Sewage Treatment Plant</td><td>Wastewater treatment</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>CFCs</b></td><td>Chlorofluorocarbons</td><td>Destroy ozone; phased out by Montreal Protocol</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>CFU</b></td><td>Colony Forming Units</td><td>E. coli limit: 0 CFU/100 mL</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>CSP</b></td><td>Concentrated Solar Power</td><td>Solar thermal power with mirrors</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>DAC</b></td><td>Direct Air Capture</td><td>Removes CO₂ from ambient air</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>DDT</b></td><td>Dichlorodiphenyltrichloroethane</td><td>POP pesticide; eggshell thinning</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>EDCs</b></td><td>Endocrine-Disrupting Chemicals</td><td>Mimic or block hormones (BPA, phthalates)</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>EGS</b></td><td>Enhanced Geothermal Systems</td><td>Fracking-based; can cause induced quakes (Basel)</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>EPI</b></td><td>Expanded Programme on Immunisation</td><td>Vaccination</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>EPR</b></td><td>Extended Producer Responsibility</td><td>Producer handles product after disposal</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>ESP</b></td><td>Electrostatic Precipitator</td><td>Charges particles onto electrodes; removes fine ash</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>FGD</b></td><td>Flue Gas Desulphurisation</td><td>Limestone scrubbers remove SO₂</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>GHG</b></td><td>Greenhouse Gas</td><td>CO₂, CH₄, N₂O, HFCs, PFCs, SF₆</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>GM</b></td><td>Genetically Modified</td><td>DNA altered by genetic engineering</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>GPP</b></td><td>Gross Primary Productivity</td><td>Total photosynthesis</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>GWP</b></td><td>Global Warming Potential</td><td>Warming relative to CO₂, usually over 100 years (GWP100)</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>HAWT / VAWT</b></td><td>Horizontal / Vertical Axis Wind Turbine</td><td>HAWT most common</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>HEPA</b></td><td>High-Efficiency Particulate Air (filter)</td><td>Pandemic control</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>HFCs / PFCs</b></td><td>Hydrofluorocarbons / Perfluorocarbons</td><td>Kyoto greenhouse gases</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>IHR</b></td><td>International Health Regulations (2005)</td><td>Binding duty to report health emergencies</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>IoT</b></td><td>Internet of Things</td><td>Connected sensors for real-time data</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>IPM</b></td><td>Integrated Pest Management</td><td>Minimises chemical pesticides</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>IRS</b></td><td>Indoor Residual Spraying</td><td>Insecticide on indoor walls</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>ISWM</b></td><td>Integrated Solid Waste Management</td><td>Combines all waste methods</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>ITNs / LLINs</b></td><td>Insecticide-Treated Nets / Long-Lasting Insecticidal Nets</td><td>Cut malaria transmission by 50%</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>IVM</b></td><td>Integrated Vector Management</td><td>ITNs + IRS + biological control</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>IWRM</b></td><td>Integrated Water Resources Management</td><td>Coordinates water, land and resources</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>JFM</b></td><td>Joint Forest Management</td><td>Community forest management</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>JI</b></td><td>Joint Implementation</td><td>Annex I to Annex I credits</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>JPOI</b></td><td>Johannesburg Plan of Implementation</td><td>2002 summit commitments</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>MAB</b></td><td>Man and the Biosphere (UNESCO)</td><td>Recognises biosphere reserves</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>MIC</b></td><td>Methyl Isocyanate</td><td>Bhopal gas</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>MINAS</b></td><td>Minimal National Standards</td><td>Effluent standards under the Water Act</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>MLD</b></td><td>Million Litres per Day</td><td>Treatment capacity unit</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>MMS</b></td><td>Moment Magnitude Scale</td><td>Earthquake size</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>MRV</b></td><td>Measuring, Reporting, Verifying</td><td>Emission accounting system from Kyoto</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>MSW</b></td><td>Municipal Solid Waste</td><td>Household garbage; India over 150,000 t/day</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>NAAQS</b></td><td>National Ambient Air Quality Standards</td><td>Set by CPCB</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>NDC</b></td><td>Nationally Determined Contribution</td><td>Each country's Paris pledge</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>NIHL</b></td><td>Noise-Induced Hearing Loss</td><td>Occupational noise damage</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>NPIs</b></td><td>Non-Pharmaceutical Interventions</td><td>Masks, distancing, ventilation</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>NPK</b></td><td>Nitrogen, Phosphorus, Potassium</td><td>Soil nutrients</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>NPP</b></td><td>Net Primary Productivity</td><td>GPP minus respiration; ~60 Pg C/yr</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>NTU</b></td><td>Nephelometric Turbidity Unit</td><td>Drinking water under 1 NTU</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>PAHs</b></td><td>Polycyclic Aromatic Hydrocarbons</td><td>Combustion pollutants</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>PAN</b></td><td>Peroxyacetyl Nitrates</td><td>Photochemical smog; eye irritation</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>PCBs</b></td><td>Polychlorinated Biphenyls</td><td>Persistent, biomagnify</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>PM2.5 / PM10</b></td><td>Particulate Matter under 2.5 / 10 µm</td><td>Key air pollutants</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>POPs</b></td><td>Persistent Organic Pollutants</td><td>DDT, Endosulfan</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>PV</b></td><td>Photovoltaic</td><td>Light to electricity directly</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>REDD+</b></td><td>Reducing Emissions from Deforestation and forest Degradation</td><td>Avoided-deforestation offsets</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>RPO</b></td><td>Renewable Purchase Obligation</td><td>Discoms must buy a share of renewables</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>SCR</b></td><td>Selective Catalytic Reduction</td><td>Ammonia + catalyst turn NOₓ into N₂</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>SDGs</b></td><td>Sustainable Development Goals</td><td>17 UN goals, 2015</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>SEA</b></td><td>Strategic Environmental Assessment</td><td>EIA for policies and plans</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>SERM</b></td><td>Selective Oestrogen Receptor Modulator</td><td>How BPA analogues act</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>SIDS</b></td><td>Small Island Developing States</td><td>Pushed for the 1.5°C target</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>SRI</b></td><td>System of Rice Intensification</td><td>Kerala; saves ~30% water</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>TDS</b></td><td>Total Dissolved Solids</td><td>Drinking water under 500 mg/L</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>ToR</b></td><td>Terms of Reference</td><td>Written at EIA scoping</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>VOCs</b></td><td>Volatile Organic Compounds</td><td>Ozone precursors (paints, solvents)</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>WASH</b></td><td>Water, Sanitation, Hygiene</td><td>Best step against waterborne disease</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>ZBNF</b></td><td>Zero Budget Natural Farming</td><td>Andhra Pradesh; mulching saves water</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>ZLD</b></td><td>Zero Liquid Discharge</td><td>Industry recycles all wastewater</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n          <h3>Schemes and missions</h3>\n          <table class=\"tbl\">\n            <thead><tr><th>Short</th><th>Full form</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td><b>FAME II</b></td><td>Faster Adoption and Manufacturing of (Hybrid and) Electric Vehicles</td><td>EV subsidy</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>GEC</b></td><td>Green Energy Corridor</td><td>Links renewables to the grid</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>GRAP</b></td><td>Graded Response Action Plan</td><td>Delhi emergency controls at AQI above 200</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>MGNREGA</b></td><td>Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Act</td><td>Funds watershed work, check dams</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>NCAP</b></td><td>National Clean Air Programme</td><td>2019; 20–30% PM cut in 122 cities</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>NSM</b></td><td>National Solar Mission</td><td>Flagship solar scale-up</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>OSOWOG</b></td><td>One Sun, One World, One Grid</td><td>Interconnect grids across time zones</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>PLI</b></td><td>Production Linked Incentive</td><td>Battery manufacturing scheme</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>PM-KUSUM</b></td><td>Pradhan Mantri Kisan Urja Suraksha evam Utthaan Mahabhiyan</td><td>Solar pumps, up to 60% subsidy</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>PMKSY</b></td><td>Pradhan Mantri Krishi Sinchayee Yojana</td><td>\"Per Drop More Crop\": drip, sprinkler</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>SAFAR</b></td><td>System of Air Quality and Weather Forecasting and Research</td><td>Real-time air quality forecasts</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n        "},{"title":"People","html":"\n          <table class=\"tbl\">\n            <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Known for</th><th>Year</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td><b>Charles Elton</b></td><td>Proposed ecological pyramids (Eltonian pyramid)</td><td>1927</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>F.E. Clements</b> (notes spell it Clemens)</td><td>Monoclimax theory: one climax set by climate</td><td>–</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Godavarman</b> (T.N. Godavarman case)</td><td>Supreme Court judgment: Forest Act covers all forest in the dictionary sense</td><td>1996</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Lindemann</b></td><td>10% law of energy transfer between trophic levels</td><td>–</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Tansley</b></td><td>Polyclimax theory: climax controlled by many factors</td><td>–</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n          <div class=\"note exam\">The notes name very few people. Companies to remember: <b>Union Carbide</b> (Bhopal, 1984) and <b>Chisso</b> (Minamata, 1956).</div>\n        "},{"title":"Places & case studies","html":"\n          <table class=\"tbl\">\n            <thead><tr><th>Place</th><th>What / why</th><th>Year</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td><b>Amazon rainforest</b></td><td>Largest tropical forest; hotspot; deforestation emissions</td><td>–</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Bhadla Solar Park</b></td><td>Largest solar park in the world</td><td>–</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Bhakra Nangal, Hirakud</b></td><td>Major Indian hydro projects</td><td>–</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Black Forest</b>, Germany</td><td>Acid rain damage</td><td>1970s–80s</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Chilika Lake</b></td><td>Lake ecosystem; Ramsar site</td><td>–</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Congo Basin</b></td><td>Second-largest tropical rainforest; hotspot</td><td>–</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Coral Triangle</b></td><td>Highest marine biodiversity</td><td>–</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Dal Lake</b></td><td>Freshwater lake ecosystem</td><td>–</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Delhi</b></td><td>AQI \"Severe\" after Diwali; GRAP</td><td>–</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Dodo</b>, Mauritius</td><td>Extinct: hunting, introduced rats, pigs, monkeys</td><td>late 1600s</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Gir NP</b>, Gujarat</td><td>Asiatic lions from under 20 to over 600</td><td>–</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Great Barrier Reef</b>, Australia</td><td>Largest reef; mass coral bleaching</td><td>–</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Gulf of Mexico</b></td><td>Dead zone ~22,720 km²</td><td>–</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Himalayas, Western Ghats, Indo-Burma, Sundaland</b></td><td>India's 4 of 36 hotspots</td><td>–</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Iceland, Kenya</b></td><td>Geothermal: over 20% and ~47% of electricity</td><td>–</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>IIT Kharagpur</b></td><td>AI map of arsenic in Ganga delta groundwater</td><td>–</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Indian Botanical Garden</b>, Kolkata</td><td>12,000+ species; Great Banyan Tree</td><td>1787</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Kalundborg</b>, Denmark</td><td>Industrial symbiosis (steam, fly ash shared)</td><td>–</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Kaziranga NP</b>, Assam</td><td>One-horned rhinoceros</td><td>–</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Keoladeo NP</b></td><td>Ramsar site</td><td>–</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Mawphlang</b>, Meghalaya</td><td>Sacred grove kept by Khasi tribes</td><td>–</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Kavus</b>, Kerala</td><td>Temple sacred groves</td><td>–</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Maldives, Tuvalu</b></td><td>Existential sea-level threat</td><td>–</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Mumbai, Chennai, Kolkata, Kochi</b></td><td>Indian cities at sea-level risk</td><td>–</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Nilgiri Biosphere Reserve</b></td><td>TN, Kerala, Karnataka; Mudumalai, Wayanad, Silent Valley</td><td>–</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>North Carolina</b>, USA</td><td>Abandoned farmland: secondary succession</td><td>–</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Oso</b>, Washington, USA</td><td>Landslide, 43 deaths</td><td>2014</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Periyar WLS</b>, Kerala</td><td>Elephants, tigers; tiger reserve</td><td>–</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Puga Valley, Tattapani, Manikaran, Cambay Basin</b></td><td>India's four geothermal regions</td><td>–</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Punjab</b></td><td>Endosulfan and DDT in groundwater</td><td>–</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Ralegan Siddhi</b>, Maharashtra</td><td>Community water management</td><td>–</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Ranipet</b>, Tamil Nadu</td><td>Chromium groundwater pollution from tanneries</td><td>–</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Rajasthan; Israel</b></td><td>Drip irrigation saves ~50%; Israel up to 80%</td><td>–</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Sardar Sarovar Dam</b></td><td>Displaced about 320,000 people</td><td>–</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Srinagar Hydro</b>, Alaknanda</td><td>Run-of-the-river, 330 MW</td><td>–</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Sundarbans</b></td><td>Mangrove forest ecosystem</td><td>–</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Taj Mahal</b></td><td>Yellowed by acid rain</td><td>–</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Tehri Dam</b>, Uttarakhand</td><td>Storage hydropower, 1,000 MW</td><td>–</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Thar Desert</b></td><td>Desert ecosystem; PM10 dust storms</td><td>–</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>The Geysers</b>, California</td><td>Largest geothermal complex; dry steam</td><td>–</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Thiruvananthapuram</b>, Kerala</td><td>Biogas from market waste</td><td>–</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Tiruppur</b></td><td>Textile water recycling</td><td>–</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Tōhoku</b>, Japan</td><td>Earthquake, tsunami, Fukushima meltdown</td><td>2011</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Yellowstone</b>, USA</td><td>Wolves reintroduced: trophic cascade</td><td>1995</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n        "},{"title":"Models, software & frameworks","html":"\n          <table class=\"tbl\">\n            <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td><b>AERMOD</b></td><td>Gaussian plume air dispersion model (EIA)</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>AQI</b></td><td>0–500 index of air quality bands</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Checklist method</b></td><td>EIA: simple, questionnaire, weighted; no cause and effect</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Digital twin</b></td><td>Virtual replica of a real system for simulation</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Doppler radar</b></td><td>Predicts cyclone paths</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>E-nose</b></td><td>AI identifies gases by chemical signature</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>EHS Insight, Envirosuite</b></td><td>Cloud EHS (environment, health, safety) platforms</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Emission factors</b></td><td>Convert activity data to CO₂e</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Enhanced Transparency Framework</b></td><td>Paris: all parties report emissions and progress</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>FlyPix AI</b></td><td>AI on satellite and drone images</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Gaussian plume</b></td><td>Air model of pollutant dispersion</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>GHG Protocol</b></td><td>Corporate carbon accounting standard</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>GIS and remote sensing</b></td><td>Map ecosystems, detect deforestation without contact</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>ISO 14001</b></td><td>Environmental Management System standard</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>ISO 14064</b></td><td>GHG inventories</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>LCA</b></td><td>Footprint from raw material to disposal</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Leopold matrix</b></td><td>EIA matrix of actions vs factors</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>LSTM</b></td><td>AI model that forecasts air quality from past data</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Mitigation hierarchy</b></td><td>Avoid, minimise, restore, offset</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Network analysis</b></td><td>EIA cause-effect chain of impacts</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>PAS 2050</b></td><td>Product carbon footprint standard</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>QUAL2K, SWAT</b></td><td>Water contamination models</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Richter scale / MMS</b></td><td>Earthquake magnitude</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Scopes 1–3</b></td><td>Direct, energy, value-chain emissions</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Sendai Framework</b></td><td>UN disaster risk framework</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n        "},{"title":"Organisms & microbes","html":"\n          <table class=\"tbl\">\n            <thead><tr><th>Organism</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead>\n            <tbody>\n              <tr><td><b>Anopheles</b> (female)</td><td>Malaria vector</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Aspergillus, Rhizopus</b></td><td>Decomposer fungi</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Azotobacter</b></td><td>Free-living nitrogen fixer</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Bacillus, Pseudomonas</b></td><td>Decomposer bacteria</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Bacillus thuringiensis</b></td><td>Bt gene; toxin against bollworms</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Bald eagle, peregrine falcon, brown pelican</b></td><td>DDT eggshell thinning</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Bengal tiger</b></td><td>Endangered; habitat loss, poaching</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Black rhino</b></td><td>Critically endangered; horn poaching</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Cyanobacteria, phytoplankton</b></td><td>Producers</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Dodo, passenger pigeon, Indian cheetah</b></td><td>Extinct examples</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>E. coli</b></td><td>Faecal indicator; 0 CFU/100 mL allowed</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Earthworms, millipedes, dung beetles</b></td><td>Detritivores</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Ganges river dolphin</b></td><td>Endangered; pollution, dams</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Giant panda, orangutan</b></td><td>Endangered (world examples)</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Gooty tarantula, Himalayan quail, Malabar civet</b></td><td>Rare species</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Indian vulture</b></td><td>Endangered by diclofenac</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Lantana camara, water hyacinth</b></td><td>Invasive plants</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Lichens and mosses</b></td><td>Pioneer species in succession</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Lion-tailed macaque, Nilgiri tahr, Andaman teal</b></td><td>Indian endemics</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Mayflies, trout, salmon</b></td><td>Pollution indicator species</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Mycorrhizal fungi</b></td><td>Mutualism with roots</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Nitrosomonas / Nitrobacter</b></td><td>Nitrification: NH₃→NO₂⁻ / NO₂⁻→NO₃⁻</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Plasmodium</b></td><td>Malaria protozoan</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Rhizobium</b></td><td>N fixation in legume root nodules</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Salmonella typhi</b></td><td>Typhoid</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>SARS-CoV-2</b></td><td>COVID-19 virus</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Sea otter, wolf</b></td><td>Keystone species</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Snow leopard, red panda</b></td><td>Himalayan hotspot species</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Vibrio cholerae</b></td><td>Cholera</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Zebra mussel, lionfish, tilapia</b></td><td>Invasive aquatic species (warm water)</td></tr>\n              <tr><td><b>Zooxanthellae</b></td><td>Coral algae expelled in bleaching</td></tr>\n            </tbody>\n          </table>\n        "}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Who proposed ecological pyramids?","back":"<b>Charles Elton</b> (1927)."},{"front":"Monoclimax vs polyclimax theory?","back":"Monoclimax: <b>Clements</b>. Polyclimax: <b>Tansley</b>."},{"front":"Expand CBDR-RC.","back":"<b>Common But Differentiated Responsibilities and Respective Capabilities</b>."}],"cardCount":12,"formulaCount":0}]});
